From 1f836a400a675d4653c2def421cfaca8c81d8b10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 20:26:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 067/206] feat(shorebird): wire the wrapper allocators, closing
 AllocateMetadata's gap

AllocateMetadata has been recorded here as having no production call site,
which is why nothing in the wrapper subsystem had ever run outside its own
tests. Their two call sites are the lambdas Deserializer::WrapperForRunTarget
returns, and both are now read out of the binary rather than guessed --
eleven instructions each, differing only in which allocator they take:

    ldr x19, [isolate_group, #0x8d0]   ; 'lambda'
    bl  AllocateMetadata
    str x20, [x0]                      ; metadata->target_entry_point = ep
    ldr x8,  [x19, #0x40]              ; layout_.rx_mapping_size
    sub x0,  x0, x8                    ; == WrapperOfMetadata(metadata)

The DWARF names the two slots -- IsolateGroup+0x8d0 is
shorebird_cpu_to_sim_wrappers_ and +0x8d8 is shorebird_sim_to_cpu_wrappers_ --
so which arm gets which is transcribed, not inferred from the control flow.
IsolateGroup grows the same pair, built on first use because each reserves
address space and a group that never loads a patch has nothing to wrap.

Note the cpu arm wraps as well. A target being native says nothing about who
calls it, and simulated code still needs a door.

Two things this turned up that reading could not have:

  * CPUToSimConfig::GetStubCodeRange still reported an empty range, with a
    comment saying the 272-byte body had not been transcribed. It had been;
    the comment outlived the work. An empty range is not inert -- EnsureStub-
    Page maps nothing, so that direction could never hand out a wrapper.
  * Two existing tests asserted exactly that emptiness, using CPUToSimConfig
    as their stand-in for "a config with no stub". They are why the stale
    range survived: the tests agreed with it. They now use a layout that is
    empty by construction, and CodeSizeComesFromTheCallback asserts both
    production configs report a real stub, so the next one to lose its stub
    fails instead of being expected.

ReadInstructions only wraps when a LinkOracle is installed. Wrapping costs a
landing pad per entry point and buys nothing without a patch. Theirs gates it
on a Deserializer field at +0xc0 whose meaning is not established, so the gate
here is the condition we can name rather than a guess at theirs.

Two tests, each with a negative control that only it catches: reporting the
CPU->Sim stub absent turns the round-trip red and leaves the config check
green; handing the cpu_to_sim accessor the Sim->CPU config does the reverse.

83 of 83 shorebird tests. verify_reproduction.sh, dd_runtime_check.sh and
dd_pass_check.sh all clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

